body:
FOLLOW-UP — the pinned runtime already exposes the lossless carrier; no fork of the parser is needed.

amr_machine.py:1171-1210 records node alignment as the actual tok_cursor indices. get_amr at :1279-1294 constructs AMR and remaps nodes AND alignments together. Therefore the runtime wrapper can, per item:
1. obtain the machine under an item-local try boundary;
2. call amr = machine.get_amr();
3. render penman = amr.to_penman(...);
4. only AFTER rendering, read amr.tokens and amr.alignments, because to_penman mutates ids via remap_ids;
5. return PENMAN plus the post-render node-id -> token-index vector sidecar.

This also gives the sibling-preserving implementation path: invoke/catch one atlas item at a time initially. The pinned public parse_sentences materializes every annotation before return, so a bad get_annotation can poison the batch (IBM #56). Fifty WOG sentences do not justify hiding that failure behind batching. We can optimize only after an equivalence court.

